ritish king of Alt Clut, the fortress on Dumbarton Rock, and an early ruler of what became the kingdom of Strathclyde.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ceretic-of-elmet"><span class="enc-name">Ceretic of Elmet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ceretic was the last known king of Elmet, a British kingdom in what is now West Yorkshire, England.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cern"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cern.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">CERN</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">CERN, the European Organization for Nuclear Research, is an intergovernmental particle physics laboratory founded in 1954 on the Franco-Swiss border near Geneva.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-azul-peru"><span class="enc-name">Cerro Azul (Peru)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cerro Azul is a coastal headland and archaeological site in the lower Cañete valley of Peru, south of Lima.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-baul"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cerro-baul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cerro Baúl</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cerro Baúl is a flat-topped mountain in the Moquegua valley of southern Peru, on whose summit the Wari Empire built a fortified colony around 600 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-de-hojas-jaboncillo"><span class="enc-name">Cerro de Hojas-Jaboncillo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cerro de Hojas-Jaboncillo is an archaeological complex on hills between Portoviejo and Montecristi in Manabí province, Ecuador.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-del-villar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cerro-del-villar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cerro del Villar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cerro del Villar is an archaeological site at the mouth of the Guadalhorce river near Málaga in southern Spain, once an island in the river's estuary.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cerro-rico.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cerro Rico</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cerro Rico ('rich hill') is a mountain above the city of Potosí in present-day Bolivia whose silver deposits were discovered by the Spanish in 1545.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cersobleptes"><span class="enc-name">Cersobleptes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cersobleptes was a son of Cotys I and the most powerful of the three kings among whom the Odrysian Kingdom was divided in 360 BCE, ruling its eastern part.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/certosa-di-pavia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/certosa-di-pavia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Certosa di Pavia</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Certosa di Pavia is a Carthusian monastery north of Pavia in Lombardy, founded in 1396 by Gian Galeazzo Visconti as a family mausoleum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/certosa-situla"><span class="enc-name">Certosa Situla</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Certosa Situla is a bronze bucket of about 500 BCE found in the 19th century in a grave of the Certosa cemetery of Etruscan Felsina, now Bologna.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cervera"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cervera.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cervera</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cervera is a town in the province of Lleida in Catalonia, Spain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cervione"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cervione.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cervione</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cervione is a hill village in the Castagniccia region of north-eastern Corsica, above the eastern coastal plain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesar-milstein"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesar-milstein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">César Milstein</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">César Milstein was an Argentine biochemist who worked mainly at Cambridge in England.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesar-vallejo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesar-vallejo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">César Vallejo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">César Vallejo was a Peruvian poet, regarded as one of the great innovators of twentieth-century poetry in any language.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesare-beccaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cesare Beccaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cesare Beccaria (1738–1794) was an Italian jurist, philosopher and economist of the Milanese Enlightenment.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesare-borgia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cesare Borgia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cesare Borgia was the son of Pope Alexander VI.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesare-d-este-duke-of-modena"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesare-d-este-duke-of-modena.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cesare d'Este, Duke of Modena</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cesare d'Este was Duke of Modena and Reggio from 1597.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesare-maria-de-vecchi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesare-maria-de-vecchi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cesare Maria De Vecchi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cesare Maria De Vecchi was an Italian Fascist leader, one of the quadrumvirs of the March on Rome in 1922.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesena"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesena.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cesena</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cesena is a city in Romagna, northern Italy, on the Savio river near the Adriatic.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cēsis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cēsis (German: Wenden) is a town in Vidzeme, northern Latvia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cesky-krumlov"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cesky-krumlov.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Český Krumlov</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Český Krumlov, in German Krumau, is a town in South Bohemia, Czech Republic, set in tight bends of the Vltava.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cestius-gallus"><span class="enc-name">Cestius Gallus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Cestius Gallus was the Roman governor of Syria from about 63 to 67 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cetina"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cetina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cetina</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cetina is a river of Dalmatia in southern Croatia, flowing about 100 kilometres from the Dinaric mountains to the Adriatic at Omiš.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cetinje"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cetinje.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cetinje</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cetinje is a town in a high karst basin of southern Montenegro and the country's old royal capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cetinje-octoechos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cetinje-octoechos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cetinje Octoechos</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cetinje Octoechos, or Oktoih, is a book of Orthodox liturgical hymns printed in 1494 by the monk Makarije at the press of Đurađ Crnojević in Cetinje.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo"><span class="enc-name">Cetshwayo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cetshwayo kaMpande was king of the Zulu from 1872 to 1879.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ceuta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ceuta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ceuta</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ceuta is a port city on the North African coast opposite Gibraltar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cevennes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cevennes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cévennes</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cévennes are a mountain range on the south-eastern edge of the Massif Central in France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ceylon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ceylon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ceylon</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ceylon is the former name of Sri Lanka, an island south of India famous for cinnamon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ceylon-independence-act-1947"><span class="enc-name">Ceylon Independence Act 1947</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ceylon Independence Act 1947 was an act of the British Parliament that granted independence to Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, as a dominion within the Commonwealth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cfa-franc"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cfa-franc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">CFA franc</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The CFA franc is the name of two currencies used in West and Central Africa, created by France in December 1945 for its African colonies.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/chol-people"><span class="enc-name">Ch'ol people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ch'ol are a Maya people whose language belongs to the Cholan branch of the Mayan family; today most live in northern Chiapas, Mexico.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaac"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chaac.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chaac</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Chaac is the Maya god of rain, lightning and storms, often shown with a long curling nose and an axe that strikes the clouds to bring rain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaamba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chaamba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chaamba</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chaamba are an Arabic-speaking nomadic tribal confederation of the northern Algerian Sahara, centred on the region of Ouargla, Metlili and El Golea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/chabrias"><span class="enc-name">Chabrias</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Chabrias was an Athenian general of the 4th century BCE and a skilled commander of mercenaries and fleets.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/chach-nama"><span class="enc-name">Chach Nama</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chach Nama is a Persian chronicle of the Arab conquest of Sindh, translated by Ali Kufi around 1226 from what it claims was an earlier Arabic work.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chaco-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chaco War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1932–1935</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chaco War was fought between Bolivia and Paraguay from 1932 to 1935 over the northern Gran Chaco, a region believed to hold oil.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Chad is a landlocked country in north-central Africa named after Lake Chad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/chadema"><span class="enc-name">Chadema</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Chadema, the Party for Democracy and Progress, is the main opposition party of mainland Tanzania, founded in 1992 when multi-party politics was restored.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chagatai-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chagatai Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Chagatai Khan was the second son of Genghis Khan, who received a share of the Mongol Empire in Central Asia and ruled from 1227 to 1242.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chagatai-khanate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chagatai Khanate</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chagatai Khanate was the Mongol successor state in Central Asia ruled by the descendants of Chagatai Khan from the thirteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-language"><span class="enc-name">Chagatai language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Chagatai is an extinct Turkic literary language of Central Asia, named after the Chagatai Khanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chaghaniyan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chaghaniyan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Chaghaniyan was a medieval principality in the valley of the Surkhan Darya, in present-day southern Uzbekistan, north of the Amu Darya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chaghri-beg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chaghri Beg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Chaghri Beg was a grandson of Seljuk and brother of Tughril, with whom he led the Seljuk Turks in the early eleventh century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chagos-archipelago"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chagos-archipelago.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chagos Archipelago</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chagos Archipelago is a group of some sixty coral islands in the central Indian Ocean, the largest being Diego Garcia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaguaramas"><span class="enc-name">Chaguaramas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Chaguaramas is a peninsula and bay at the north-western tip of Trinidad, west of Port of Spain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chahar-lang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chahar-lang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chahar Lang</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chahar Lang is one of the two main divisions of the Bakhtiari tribal confederation of south-western Iran, the other being the Haft Lang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/chahar-mongols"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/chahar-mongols.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Chahar Mongols</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Chahar are a Mongol people of Inner Mongolia.</span></a></li></ul>
